// SQL-generation CSD for Spider dataset. Key observations from the ledger:
// 1. Best attempt (41.7% acc, 84.3% syntax) used simple unconstrained-until-"<<"
//    then AdaptiveConstrainedStep. The main failure modes are:
//    - 128 syntax-valid but semantically wrong answers (query structure ok, wrong tables/columns)
//    - 24 malformed spans (invalid SQL tokens like semicolons, backticks, {})
//    - 61 missing spans (model never emits "<<")
//
// 2. The "entered_constrained_mode_too_early" pattern (238 examples) shows
//    the model emits "<<" almost immediately after "SQL: " which is GOOD behavior
//    - the evaluator calls it a failure label but the output tail examples show
//    correct format. The real problem is semantic accuracy.
//
// 3. To improve: 
//    a. Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep to discourage token repetition in SQL
//    b. Use GroupBoostedConstrainedStep to boost schema tokens (table/column names)
//    c. Alternate between boosted and penalty steps based on position in query
//    d. Use CloseSpanWithinBudget to guarantee span closure
//    e. Better guidance to orient the model toward correct SQL structure
//
// Strategy:
//   Phase 0: unconstrained until "<<" (model typically does this quickly)
//   Phase 1: inside constrained span, use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep combined
//     with GroupBoostedConstrainedStep (alternating or based on parser state)
//     to generate accurate, non-repetitive SQL. Use CloseSpanWithinBudget
//     to guarantee the span closes.
//
// The key improvement vs best: use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep to avoid the
// repeated token patterns seen in malformed outputs, and use stronger boosts
// on the schema tokens from validTokenGroups.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   Outside-span branch: implication is vacuous. When next == "<<", we set
//     currentConstrainedOut := [] which satisfies parser.IsValidPrefix([]).
//   CloseConstrainedSpan branch: sets insideConstrainedOut := false, making
//     implication vacuous; currentConstrainedOut := [].
//   CloseSpanWithinBudget branch: by its contract returns either closed span
//     (insideConstrainedOut false, currentConstrainedOut []) or still-open
//     valid prefix. Both satisfy the invariant.
//   Constrained-step branch: SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep returns EOS or a
//     parser-valid next token. GroupBoostedConstrainedStep similarly. 
//     AppendConstrainedToken extends currentConstrainedOut by one valid token,
//     preserving parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut).
//
// progress:
//   Every loop iteration in phase 0 increments steps by 1 and appends at most
//   one visible token, so |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps.
//   Every loop iteration in phase 1 increments steps by 1 and appends at most
//   one visible token via AppendConstrainedToken.
//   CloseConstrainedSpan costs 1 step and appends at most one delimiter ">>"
//   CloseSpanWithinBudget costs at most closeBudget steps; we set steps := maxSteps
//   afterward. By CloseSpanWithinBudget postcondition |generated| <= old|generated| + closeBudget
//   = old|generated| + (maxSteps - steps_before_close) <= |generatedPrefix| + maxSteps.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var guidance: string := "Generate exactly one SQL query matching the schema. Output format: SQL: <<your SQL query>>. Use only tables and columns from the schema. No explanation, no markdown.";
